{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a film that explores the origins of facebook and the consequences that followed its creation. the movie is based on the book \"the accidental billionaires\" by ben mezrich and was adapted for the screen by aaron sorkin. the film follows mark zuckerberg, a harvard undergraduate, as he creates a website called facemash, which compares the photos of two students and asks users to vote on which one is more attractive.\n\nafter the success of facemash, mark is approached by twin brothers cameron and tyler winklevoss, who propose an idea for a social networking site exclusively for harvard students. mark agrees to work on the project, but eventually drops the twins and teams up with his friend eduardo saverin to create a similar site called thefacebook.\n\nas thefacebook gains popularity, mark and eduardo become wealthy and famous, but their friendship begins to deteriorate. the twins, who claim that mark stole their idea, sue him for theft and intellectual property theft. the film also explores the relationship between mark and his girlfriend erica albright, who eventually breaks up with him due to his increasing obsession with thefacebook.\n\nthe social network features a talented cast, including jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, and justin timberlake as sean parker, the co-founder of napster. the film's cinematography is also noteworthy, with a muted color palette that reflects the dark and moody tone of the story.\n\none of the most impressive aspects of the social network is its script, which was written by aaron sorkin. the dialogue is witty and engaging, and the characters are well-developed and complex. the film's themes of ambition, greed, and the consequences of one's actions are also thought-provoking and timely.\n\noverall, the social network is a well-crafted and engaging film that ex